{\rtlch\af0\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\fs24\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\f0 https://unsplash.com/@ashni_ahlawat}{\*\datafield 08d0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b0200000003000000e0c9ea79f9bace118c8200aa004ba90b48000000680074007400700073003a002f002f0075006e00730070006c006100730068002e0063006f006d002f0040006100730068006e0069005f00610068006c0061007700610074000000}}
{\fldrslt{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf1 Ashni}}}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0
\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 /Unsplash]}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0
\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 Never, ever bother correcting him,\u8221  groupies posted of Mikkelson online. \u8220 He *knows* he\u8217 s wrong, he just wants you to admit it. Just follow his trail of devastation. . . . It can be quite fun to watch.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 David liked [newcomers] with ketchup,\u8221  Tepper told me, which, in non-AFU-speak, means he regarded them as easy prey, like snacks. Mikkelson seemed to think of himself as an iconoclast who merited respect, and who harbored resentment toward others whom he found undeserving of their status\u8212 a feeling that The New Yorker\u8217 s then-new editor-in-chief Tina Brown also seemed to inspire.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In 1993, Mikkelson trolled Brown by snail mail: \u8220 I was wondering if you could be prevailed upon to change the name of your magazine. I keep confusing it with a very fine publication of identical title that mysteriously disappeared from newsstands several months ago.\u8221  In this case, he signed his own name, not his AFU username: \u8220 snopes.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Mikkelson had adopted a surname of William Faulkner\u8217 s creation, a family \u8220 of pure sons of bitches,\u8221  who appear in a number of Faulkner\u8217 s works. Mikkelson was very familiar with their saga: Flem Snopes, the central character, possessed a talent for verisimilitude, which helped him climb from outcast sharecropper to bank president and church deacon.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Mikkelson would go on to say that he had chosen snopes \u8220 simply because it was short and distinctive,\u8221  and he\u8217 d only shrug noncommittally when asked if he was a fan of Faulkner\u8217 s. But people have often pondered the connection to an alias along the lines of snipe, snicker, sneak, or snake. \u8220 We all felt that it was deeply, deeply appropriate,\u8221  says Teasley.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Mikkelson was one of their \u8220 best trolls\u8221  and a \u8220 great researcher,\u8221  another core AFUer tells me over Twitter DM. \u8220 But he was never a good guy, just high status, given what we valued back then (exclusivity, sarcasm, laying traps for people to walk into).\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In 1994, Mikkelson and Barbara, now married, launched Snopes.com. Was the website his idea or hers, I had asked him when we first spoke, years ago\u8212 and when did Snopes begin? \u8220 When did the Beatles begin?\u8221  he answered. \u8220 The day Paul first played with them? The day Ringo joined? How do you pick a point on a continuum?\u8221  That feels so true. Information gets awfully mutable when it\u8217 s fitted to a story. Wasn\u8217 t that the revelatory nature of their whole operation? (I reached out to Mikkelson recently, but he didn\u8217 t respond to my request for further comment.)}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Barbara recalled the early days: \u8220 Instead of being cast in stone, our articles evolved as older legends reappeared with new twists to them, which was very exciting,\u8221  she said. \u8220 Without realizing we\u8217 d be doing so, we found ourselves recording what America was feeling, in that the urban legends in current circulation present a fairly accurate reflection of what the country is, at that moment, worrying about.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Ex-Snopes chief operating officer Vinny Green (who would himself figure heavily in the site\u8217 s downward trajectory) says the Mikkelsons were ahead of their time, with \u8220 the best idea for 2016 in 1996.\u8221  Its scope, though, did not creep out until later, after the attacks of September 11 in 2001, at which point the myth-busting mom-and-pop concern geared into a more political address, debunking false claims like the narrative that Palestinians danced in the streets after the terrorist attack on the World Trade Center. Whatever their visitors thought Snopes\u8217  remit should be, they were looking to it for \u8220 something else.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The Mikkelsons were not trained fact-checkers. They did not think what they were doing was journalism, but they tried to behave like journalists and often discussed journalistic standards in suburban L.A., where they worked at opposite ends of a home shared with cats and pet rats.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 She ran the brand. He ran the backend. They had beats\u8212 she liked to cover business, crime, glurge, sports, and soft drink trivia; he preferred Disney, music, movies, radio, and TV\u8212 and they more or less split the research and writing. Mikkelson joined Barbara full time in 2002 (after he was laid off from his programming job). Nothing posted before it was reviewed by the other.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In March 2002, Snopes published an article purporting to quash a claim made by Michael Moore that members of bin Laden\u8217 s family had flown during the U.S.\u8217 s travel ban in the days after 9/11. \u8220 This just goes to show what a little bit of fact flipped onto its side and then spewed by a public figure can do,\u8221  Barbara wrote on Snopes at the time.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Come September 2003, Moore seemed not to have been so off the mark after all, and Snopes overhauled its article but failed to mention the significant corrections\u8212 an oversight for which it was roundly criticized. Though Barbara apologized, some complained that Snopes had swept sensitive errors under the rug before, and watchdogs pointed out that the site had, apparently that same week, coded in a noindex meta tag\u8212 which meant Google crawlers could not cache Snopes\u8217  pages. Barbara says she was not consulted and was not aware of that decision, which might have had an innocent explanation. It smelled, however, like a way to preempt getting confronted with revisionism, right when the digital presentation of edits was a hot topic.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 (In fact, since November 2002, Mikkelson had also excluded Snopes from the Internet Archive\u8217 s Wayback Machine, a project by the digital library to snapshot the World Wide Web for future reference. Mark Graham noticed the exclusion when he started as the library\u8217 s director in 2015. \u8220 I was like, What the fuck,\u8221  Graham says.)}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Snopes predated FactCheck.org, created by the Annenberg Public Policy Center in 2003, and PolitiFact.com, created in 2007 by the Tampa Bay Times\u8212 now run by the Poynter Institute. But unlike those outlets, Snopes\u8217  identity was never quite clear. Was it a fact-checking newsroom or a place for irony-laced commentary? Or something in between? And wasn\u8217 t Snopes entitled to growing pains?}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 It might feel unfair to expect a pillbox to keep up with the papers of record, but less so as it left the realm of hobby to be a media business in earnest. \u8220 I wouldn\u8217 t give them a Get Out of Jail Free card just because they started on a listserv somewhere,\u8221  says Mathew Ingram, a digital media reporter at Columbia Journalism Review.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 By 2010, the Mikkelsons were spending much of their bandwidth on political claims\u8212 even if politics, they told the New York Times, was the last thing they wished to be writing about.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24
\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 Barbara had never heard the backstory behind a password the Mikkelsons used for various Snopes accounts: \u8220 henry8wives.\u8221  In 2010, in Las Vegas, Mikkelson met a professional escort named Elyssa Young. According to Young (who\u8217 d eventually join\~Snopes as an administrative assistant), Mikkelson said the password had once come assigned to a piece of hardware, and he joked that the reference to the English king\u8217 s serial marriages augured his own future.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In 2014, Barbara discovered Mikkelson\u8217 s affair with Young and left him. By that point, poor health had obliged her to write far less: She authored 132 articles from 2011-2014, of a total\u8212 by her count\u8212 of 1,905. Three months later, Mikkelson brought Kim LaCapria on as the site\u8217 s first outside writer. LaCapria knew the internet\u8217 s dark caves and had even met her husband, in the 2000s, on the message board that Snopes once hosted. \u8220 I felt like I was channeling Barbara\u8217 s voice,\u8221  she said.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In November 2015, Brooke Binkowski, a high-octane border and post-conflict reporter, came on as the site\u8217 s first managing editor to help take Snopes from folksy to pro in time for the presidential election. More writers followed. And Mikkelson and Young stayed together, eventually moving to Tacoma, Washington, where they married, and where, in Mikkelson\u8217 s home office, an old clothbound set of Faulkner\u8217 s Snopes novels served as a laptop stand.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 The term \u8220 fake news\u8221  was being popularized just as Snopes was professionalizing its shop. But Mikkelson appears to have been struggling to keep up the good fight. The site had grown ramshackle, with especially poor load times on articles. More importantly, it seems Mikkelson still didn\u8217 t get that if you\u8217 re fact-checking, you\u8217 re doing journalism\u8212 so you have to play by all the rules. It would later come out that Mikkelson had a plagiarism habit; the earliest example of that in a Snopes article\u8212 \u8220 Are McDonald\u8217 s Minions Toys Swearing?\u8221 \u8212 dates to July 2015, which was also the year of the Mikkelsons\u8217  bitter divorce, halving the ownership of Snopes between them.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 It was around this time that two twentysomething entrepreneurs\u8212 tech wiz Chris Richmond and Drew Schoentrup, an intellectual property attorney\u8212 grew interested in acquiring Snopes. Mikkelson later told me that selling his half of Snopes to them was \u8220 never on the table,\u8221  but in an email to Schoentrup, in June, he\u8217 d listed it as one of the possibilities to which he was \u8220 open.\u8221  He went on: \u8220 Honestly, I\u8217 m not a 20-something looking to become the next Mark Zuckerberg and make a gazillion dollars with an online empire. If I can just recoup what I\u8217 ve lost to Barbara, then I\u8217 d be happy and have no concerns about who owns or manages things.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 What ended up happening, Richmond and Schoentrup started an ad tech firm, Proper Media, to handle Snopes\u8217  advertising and infrastructure, including an overdue move to the WordPress CMS. (Snopes is mostly funded through its digital ad sales.)}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 But soon, Mikkelson encouraged Richmond and Schoentrup to buy Barbara out. \u8220 Keep in mind that although she may sound rational, Barbara has a personality }{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf2 disorder}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2  and a sometimes tenuous grasp of reality,\u8221  he cautioned Schoentrup in a Slack message sent in January 2016. Barbara, who denies any such }
{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b\i\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ul\cf2 disorder}{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0
\ulnone\cf2 , agreed to sell.}{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 In December 2015, Binkowski messaged her boss that she was editing the site\u8217 s About Page. Mikkelson promptly instructed her, \u8220 Don\u8217 t mention Barbara . . .\u8221  Eventually, the page, which had previously stated that the site was \u8220 founded by Barbara and David Mikkelson,\u8221  a \u8220 team,\u8221  would state simply that it was \u8220 founded by David Mikkelson.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Articles written pre-WordPress had no byline, but Barbara had always signed her work in the footer, using wry internyms (\u8220 Barbara \u8216 the power behind the throne\u8217  Mikkelson\u8221 ) in the mode of their AFU days. Six weeks before she sold her half of the company to Richmond and Schoentrup, Mikkelson told Binkowski that his ex\u8217 s signatures could be deleted; and that summer, on the day the $3.6 million sale was inked, Binkowski Slacked Mikkelson to say she was removing Barbara\u8217 s name from everything she found. \u8220 Is that all right?\u8221  Binkowski asked again, according to captures reviewed by Fast Company. \u8220 Yes, please,\u8221  he replied.}
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 When Snopes migrated to WordPress, articles were assigned by default to \u8220 Snopes.\u8221  But that byline was changed to give Barbara\u8217 s articles to Mikkelson specifically. \u8220 David\u8217 s byline wasn\u8217 t added to those pages until way later,\u8221  said LaCapria, who helped Binkowski to scrub Barbara\u8217 s name. (They secretively kept her name buried in the HTML, invisible to the casual reader, but still there and part of the site\u8217 s records). The reattribution came up in a Slack exchange with the one-time COO Green in December 2016: \u8220 It\u8217 s not a big deal,\u8221  Mikkelson said. \u8220 I should take credit for everything she wrote just to spite her.\u8221 }
{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033
\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 \u8220 That should have been a red flag,\u8221  says CJR\u8217 s Ingram, referring to Barbara\u8217 s removal. \u8220 It seemed so petty.\u8221 }{\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24\par}\pard\plain\itap0\s0\fi0\sb200\sa0
\aspalpha\aspnum\adjustright\ltrpar\li0\lin0\ri0\rin0\qj\faauto\sl260\slmult0\rtlch\afs24\ltrch\fs24{\rtlch\af2\alang1025\afs24\ltrch\b0\i0\fs20\lang1033\langnp1033\langfe1033\langfenp1033\loch\af2\dbch\af2\hich\f2\strike0\ulnone\cf2 Donald Trump had just been elected the month prior. For the first time, \u8220 journalists had to start reacting to lies rather than just seeking truths in the Trump era,\u8221  says Emily Dreyfuss, senior managing editor of the Technology and Social Change team at Harvard\u8217 s Shorenstein Center for Media, Politics, and Public Policy.\~(In 2017, The Washington Post, for example, created a \u8220 Fact Checker\u8217 s database\u8221  to track President Trumps\u8217  false or misleading claims; it had logged 30,573 such claims by the end of his term.) Yet, here was Snopes, a site that tasked itself with helping people to verify reality, spinning its own history, courtesy of Mikkelson.}
